/*
time - o(nlogn) - sorting pq
space - o(n)
intuition
1.Find how long each car needs to reach the target.

Cars with large (target - position) or small speed take longer.
This time tells you which cars can catch up.

2. Put all cars into a priority queue sorted by position.

This ensures you process cars from closest to the target → farthest.
You always want to check if a car behind can catch the car in front.

3. Start popping cars one by one (from closest forward).

Each popped car gives you the time it will take to reach the target.

4. Use a stack to keep track of “fleets” by time.

If the current car takes less time, it forms a new fleet → push

If it takes equal/more time, it catches the fleet ahead → pop that fleet

This works because a slower/late-arriving car gets stuck behind the fleet in front.

5. Final number of fleet times in the stack = number of car fleets.

*/




class Solution {
    public int carFleet(int target, int[] position, int[] speed) {
        PriorityQueue<float[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a,b)->Float.compare(a[0],b[0]));
        Stack<Float> times = new Stack();
        int n = position.length;
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
            float t = (float)(target - position[i])/speed[i];
            pq.add(new float[]{position[i],t});
        }
        while(!pq.isEmpty()){
            float cur = pq.poll()[1];
            while(!times.isEmpty() && times.peek()<=cur){
                times.pop();
            }
            times.push(cur);
        }
        return times.size();

    }
}
